1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,182
Lesson 8.4.

2
00:00:02,562 --> 00:00:03,162
K-FAC.

3
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:08,650
Even with tricks, the full natural
gradient is expensive for large networks.

4
00:00:09,030 --> 00:00:13,322
K-FAC, proposed by Martens and Grosse
in two thousand fifteen, makes two

5
00:00:13,322 --> 00:00:18,151
approximations: it ignores correlations
between different layers, and within each

6
00:00:18,151 --> 00:00:22,681
layer it factors the curvature as a
Kronecker product of two small matrices.

7
00:00:23,061 --> 00:00:28,093
The result is a second-order optimizer
almost as cheap as a first-order one.

8
00:00:29,217 --> 00:00:33,384
In a linear layer, the gradient
with respect to the weights is the

9
00:00:33,384 --> 00:00:37,552
outer product of the input activation
a and the output gradient g.

10
00:00:37,932 --> 00:00:44,476
The layer's Fisher block is then the
expected value of (a aᵀ) ⊗ (g gᵀ).

11
00:00:44,856 --> 00:00:47,675
K-FAC assumes activations
and gradients are

12
00:00:47,675 --> 00:00:50,364
independent and approximates it by A ⊗ G,

13
00:00:50,744 --> 00:00:56,238
with A = E[a aᵀ] and G = E[g gᵀ].

14
00:00:57,350 --> 00:00:59,105
The advantage is in the inversion.

15
00:00:59,485 --> 00:01:05,972
The inverse of a Kronecker product is the
product of the inverses, and applying it

16
00:01:05,972 --> 00:01:11,588
to a gradient is G⁻¹ ∇W A⁻¹: two
small matrices instead of a giant one.

17
00:01:11,968 --> 00:01:16,464
For a layer with twenty inputs and
ten outputs, instead of inverting a

18
00:01:16,464 --> 00:01:20,639
two-hundred-by-two-hundred matrix
one inverts a twenty and a ten.

19
00:01:21,019 --> 00:01:23,499
For thousand-by-thousand
layers, one goes from a

20
00:01:23,499 --> 00:01:26,548
million-by-million matrix to
two thousand-by-thousand ones.

21
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:30,922
We measure it on a softmax layer
with five hundred examples.

22
00:01:31,302 --> 00:01:33,878
The Kronecker approximation
differs from the

23
00:01:33,878 --> 00:01:36,336
exact block by twenty-one percent in norm.

24
00:01:36,716 --> 00:01:42,962
But what matters is the step direction:
K-FAC's natural gradient has a cosine of

25
00:01:42,962 --> 00:01:48,428
0.94 with the exact one, while the
ordinary gradient only reaches 0.5.

26
00:01:48,808 --> 00:01:52,590
The matrix is approximate;
the direction is almost right.

27
00:01:53,717 --> 00:01:57,201
In practice, the factors
A and G are estimated with

28
00:01:57,201 --> 00:02:01,231
exponential moving averages
across iterations, and inverted

29
00:02:01,231 --> 00:02:04,852
only every so many steps,
because they change slowly.

30
00:02:05,232 --> 00:02:10,788
Damping is split between the two factors,
adding a square root of λ to each.

31
00:02:11,168 --> 00:02:15,177
That way, the extra cost compared
with Adam is small and amortized.

32
00:02:16,283 --> 00:02:21,453
Modern layers share weights: a
convolution uses the same filter at every

33
00:02:21,453 --> 00:02:25,833
position, and a transformer,
the same matrix for every token.

34
00:02:26,213 --> 00:02:29,590
Eschenhagen and colleagues
proposed two variants.

35
00:02:29,970 --> 00:02:33,684
K-FAC expand treats each position
as one more example in the batch.

36
00:02:34,064 --> 00:02:37,862
K-FAC reduce first sums
activations and gradients over the

37
00:02:37,862 --> 00:02:41,463
positions and then forms the
factors, which is cheaper.

38
00:02:41,843 --> 00:02:46,308
Both are exact in particular linear
cases and work well in practice.

39
00:02:47,417 --> 00:02:52,177
K-FAC is used to train convolutional
networks and transformers with second-order

40
00:02:52,177 --> 00:02:56,641
information, and also for Laplace
approximations that estimate uncertainty.

41
00:02:57,021 --> 00:03:00,432
Its bottleneck is inverting the
factors, and that is what the

42
00:03:00,432 --> 00:03:03,396
thermodynamic version of
the next lesson accelerates.

43
00:03:04,517 --> 00:03:08,869
Common mistakes: forgetting that K-FAC
ignores curvature between layers.

44
00:03:09,249 --> 00:03:11,672
Applying the factors in the wrong order.

45
00:03:12,052 --> 00:03:13,973
Damping only one of the two.

46
00:03:14,353 --> 00:03:17,808
And mixing the statistics of
the expand and reduce variants.

47
00:03:18,917 --> 00:03:22,875
In summary: K-FAC approximates
each curvature block by a Kronecker

48
00:03:22,875 --> 00:03:26,773
product of the activation covariance
and the gradient covariance,

49
00:03:26,773 --> 00:03:29,892
which turns one giant
inversion into two small ones.

50
00:03:30,272 --> 00:03:34,498
In the example, the matrix has
a twenty-one percent error, but

51
00:03:34,498 --> 00:03:38,521
the step direction has a cosine
of 0.94 with the exact one.

52
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:43,280
To practice: prove the inverse
of the Kronecker product.

53
00:03:43,660 --> 00:03:46,092
Verify the identity with vectorization.

54
00:03:46,472 --> 00:03:48,743
Compute the cost of inverting the factors.

55
00:03:49,123 --> 00:03:51,431
And explain the independence assumption.
